%N A398834 Number of positive integer divisors of (3^n-1)/2.
%C A398834 Let I[x] denote the set of polynomials p = p(x) all of whose coefficients are integers. For p(x) in I[x], denote by f(x,i), for i=1..m, the irreducible divisors in I[x] of p(x). Let c be an integer.  The numbers f(c,i), together with 1, are here called type 1 (p,c)-divisors of p(c).  The products of two or more of the numbers f(c,i) that are divisors of p(c) are type 2 (p,c)-divisors of p(c); and all other divisors of p(c) are type 3 (p,c)-divisors. These definitions generalize naturally to polynomials p(x,y) in two variables.
%C A398834 Example 1. p(x) = (x + 1)(x^2 + 1). The (p,3)-divisors of p(3)=40 of types 1,2, and 3 are {1, 4, 10}, {40), and  {2,4,8,20}, respectively.
%C A398834 Example 2. p(x,y) = x^2 + x*y + y^2. The (p, (5,2))-divisors of p(5,2)=39 of types 1,2, and 3 are {1, 39}, the empty set, and {3,13}, respectively.
%C A398834 For this sequence, the a(n) divisors of (3^n - 1)/2, for n = 1..7 are partitioned into the 3 types as shown below. Note that primes can occur in both types 1 or 3, or both, depending on n.
%C A398834 n    type 1            type 2                    type 3
%C A398834 1    1                 none                       none
%C A398834 2    1,4               none                       2
%C A398834 3    1,13              none                       none
%C A398834 4    1,4,10            40                         2,5,8,20
%C A398834 5    1,121             none                       11
%C A398834 6    1,4,7,13          28,52,91,364               2,4,16,182
%C A398834 7    1,1093            none                       none
%C A398834 In the following guide to related sequences, column 1 shows (x,y), followed by A-numbers for the number of divisors of (x^n - y^n)/(x - y); number of squarefree divisors, least prime divisor, and greatest prime divisor.
%C A398834 (x,y)   #Divisors     #SF Div       LP Div        GP Div
%C A398834 (2,1)   A046801       A049093       A049479       A005420
%C A398834 (3,1)   A398834       A398835       A399476       A074477
%C A398834 (3,2)   A399911       A400720       A089082       A400721
%C A398834 (4,1)   A400722       A259178       A400723       A274906
%C A398834 (4,3)   A400724       A400725       A400726       A400727
%e A398834 The a(6) = 12 divisors of (3^6 - 1)/2 = 364 are 1, 2, 4, 7, 13, 14, 26, 28, 52, 91, 182, 364.
%t A398834 {x, y} = {3, 1}; p[n_] := p[n] = (x^n - y^n)/(x - y);
%t A398834 Table[DivisorSigma[0, p[n]], {n, 1, 100}]
%o A398834 (PARI) a(n) = numdiv((3^n-1)/2); \\ _Michel Marcus_, Nov 11 2026
